Encode a shared screen once for every connection

Every connection that sends a track has a VideoStreamEncoder of its own, and each made
an encoder of its own from the factory: a screen shared to seven watchers was encoded
seven times, with seven libvpx encoders, their buffers and their threads, although
every one of them is given the same frames (VideoBroadcaster hands each sink the same
VideoFrame) at the same settings.

With the field trial Fogata-SharedScreenEncode/Enabled/, the factory's VP8 encoders
are proxies (src/internal/shared_encoder.{h,cc}). A source marked with
RTCVideoSource::EnableSharedEncode tags its frames (VideoFrame::id: a source number
from 1 to 15 and a 12-bit count); proxies given frames of one source at the same
settings (those VideoStreamEncoder's RequiresEncoderReset compares) form a group with
one real encoder on a task queue of its own. A frame is encoded once when any member
asks for it, and the image is given to every member in sync, with the RTP timestamp
its own VideoStreamEncoder gave or would have given the frame; a member skips no image
from a key frame on, so each connection's picture ids stay consecutive. Key frames
asked for are shared and at most one a second; rates are the lowest live member's; a
member far slower than the others, or one asking for key frames again and again, gets
an encoder of its own. Untagged frames (cameras), simulcast and other codecs are
encoded as before. Every connection still packetizes, encrypts, paces and estimates on
its own: nothing on the wire changes.

Tested by test/shared_encoder.test.cc.

